  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThe current algorithm uses binary representations and local search methods to minimize the overlap integral. While effective, this approach might be constrained by the binary nature. A different perspective: the Erds minimum overlap problem can be viewed as a separable variable optimization with constraints. \n\nOur strategy is based on:\n1) Smooth representation using Slepian sequences for optimal energy concentration\n2) Block programming with careful time budget monitoring\n3) Multi-scale optimization anchored to parent data\n\nThe key innovation is to use Slepian representations because these functions (eigenfunctions of autocorrelation) concentrate energy in both time and frequency domains, natural for minimizing shift-overlap. We will represent h as linear combinations of these sequences while maintaining feasibility.\n\nThe optimization will be gradient-based with augmented Lagrangian for constraints (\u222bh=1, 0\u2264h\u22641). \n\nMathematical specification:\n\nDefine basis sequences (u_i, v_i) such that \u222bu_i \u03c6 = \u222bv_i \u03c6 for all test functions \u03c6 (orthonormal in L2(0,2)). We use Fourier bases but with cut-off frequencies for smooth representation.\n\nOptimization variables: coefficients x_i such that h = \u03a3 (a_i u_i + b_i v_i)\n\nConstraints:\n1. \u222bh dx = 1\n2. 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n\nObjective: minimize max_k \u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nWe will use:\n- CNN for approximate gradient evaluation\n- Augmented Lagrangian for exact constraints\n- Line search for global improvement\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - Set n=400 if parent available\n   - If parent exists, convert to Slepian coefficients\n   - Otherwise initialize randomly\n\n2. Augmented Lagrangian Implementation:\n   - Define cost = max_k integral overlap\n   - Define constraint residual R = \u222bh - 1 and domain violation D = max(|h|-1)\n   - Use Slepian representations for basis approximation\n\n3. Optimization Steps:\n   a) Compute gradients via central differences for CNN\n   b) Perform line search with Armijo condition\n   c) Update potentials with stabilized Newton method\n   d) Introduce soft constraints via projection\n\n4. Time Budget Management:\n   - Allocate phases based on moments of day\n   - Monitor wall-clock time\n   - Use response surface methodology to avoid function evaluations\n\n5. Parameter Management:\n   - Number of bases B = 100\n   - Penalty parameters \u03c1 proportional to \u221a(target_cost)\n   - Step length adaptation based on trust region\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n\n1. Basis selection variations: use wavelets, Gabor filters\n2. Adaptive basis number: start at B=0, increase until observation stops help\n3. Downhill/uphill cycles with budget adaptation\n4. Sensitivity analysis for coefficient magnitudes\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n\n1. Dominant costs: gradient estimation (n=400, \u223c0.5s per eval)\n2. Timetable: snap checks every 3s, re-evaluate objectives at 15min intervals\n3. Fallback strategy: \n   - Save best solution at intervals\n   - Use convex fallback when budget tightens\n   - Maintain baseline performance if optimization fails\n\nValidation and return contract:\n\n1. Input-output contract:\n   - accept sequences of four phases with objectives\n   - produce (h_values, c5, n_points)\n\n2. Final checks:\n   - Re-verify \u222bh=1 to tolerance 1e-5\n   - Re-wind to n=400 if downscaled\n   - Return best solution ever found\n\n3. Industry vetting:\n   - Integrate with online solver slack\n   - Use response surfaces for communication evaluation",
